/* TIH — Authored per-topic teaching notes for the Android app development course.
   The course player renders these directly instead of the generic lesson
   template. Keyed by topic title; numbering, leading emoji and case are
   ignored when matching. A key may be module-qualified ("M9:Title"). */
(function () {
  if (typeof window === 'undefined') return;
  window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES = window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES || {};
  window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES['android'] = {
  "Welcome to the Course": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Welcome to the Course</strong><br><span>From no code at all to an app published on Google Play.</span></div><p>This course teaches Android development in <strong>Kotlin</strong>, the language Google has recommended since 2019, using <strong>Android Studio</strong>, the official development environment.</p><p>The order matters. Modules 2 and 3 give you the language and the tools; 4 to 11 build apps that do real work; 12 to 14 cover security, testing and publishing; 15 to 18 add AI tooling, freelancing and the projects that become your portfolio.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Kotlin is the primary language; Java still runs but new work starts in Kotlin.</li><li><strong>Android Studio</strong> is the official IDE and is free.</li><li>You need a computer, not a phone, to develop &mdash; the emulator replaces a device.</li><li>Every module ends with a quiz you must pass to unlock the next.</li><li>Modules 17 and 18 are eleven real apps; they are the portfolio, not extras.</li><li>Type every example yourself. Reading code teaches far less than running it.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A learner in Monrovia finishing this course has eleven working apps and a published Play Store listing. That listing is what a client or employer actually looks at &mdash; more than any certificate.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Android development is one of the few skills where the work can be done from anywhere and sold anywhere. The portfolio is what makes that possible.</p></div>",
  "What is Android App Development?": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 What is Android App Development?</strong><br><span>Writing software for the operating system on most of the world's phones.</span></div><p>Android is an open-source operating system built on the Linux kernel and used by the large majority of smartphones worldwide. Android development means writing applications that run on it.</p><p>A modern Android app is built from a few standard pieces: <strong>activities</strong> and screens the user sees, <strong>layouts</strong> that arrange them, code that responds to input, and often a network or database layer behind it.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Android is open source and built on the Linux kernel.</li><li>It holds the majority of global smartphone market share, especially outside North America.</li><li>Apps are distributed through Google Play and other stores, or installed directly.</li><li><strong>Native</strong> Android means Kotlin or Java; cross-platform tools compile to it.</li><li>Each app runs in its own sandbox with its own permissions.</li><li>Devices vary hugely in screen size, Android version and performance.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">That variety is the defining constraint. An app that works on your emulator must also work on a five-year-old phone with a small screen and an intermittent connection &mdash; which describes a large share of the devices in Liberia.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Designing for that reality rather than for a flagship device is the difference between an app that gets used and one that gets uninstalled.</p></div>",
  "Career Opportunities in Android Development": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Career Opportunities in Android Development</strong><br><span>Employment, freelancing, or your own product &mdash; and all three are remote-capable.</span></div><p>Android skills lead to three routes: employment with a company, freelance contract work, or building and publishing your own app. They demand different things, and you can move between them.</p><p>For someone in Liberia, freelancing is often the most accessible entry: it needs no relocation, no local employer, and the client cares about your portfolio rather than your address.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Employment</strong>: steady income, mentorship, slower start.</li><li><strong>Freelance</strong>: accessible remotely; portfolio matters more than credentials.</li><li><strong>Own product</strong>: highest ceiling, longest and least certain path.</li><li>Employers ask for published apps and readable code far more than certificates.</li><li>Related roles: QA, mobile UI design, technical support, app maintenance.</li><li>Maintenance contracts on existing apps are steady, underrated work.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A common first freelance job is not building an app from nothing. It is fixing, updating or re-publishing someone's existing app &mdash; which is exactly what Modules 13 and 14 prepare you for.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Knowing the routes tells you what to build. If freelancing is the target, the portfolio in Modules 17 and 18 matters more than any other part of this course.</p></div>",
  "Android Ecosystem Overview": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Android Ecosystem Overview</strong><br><span>The pieces around your code: the OS, the store, the services and the tools.</span></div><p>Your app sits inside a larger system. <strong>Android OS</strong> provides the runtime and APIs. <strong>Google Play</strong> distributes it. <strong>Play Services</strong> supply maps, sign-in, payments and more. <strong>Firebase</strong> offers a backend without you running servers.</p><p>API levels matter. Each Android release has a number, and your app declares the minimum it supports. Set it too high and you exclude older devices; too low and you cannot use newer features without extra work.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>API level</strong> identifies the Android version your app targets.</li><li><em>minSdk</em> is the oldest device you support; <em>targetSdk</em> is what you build against.</li><li>Google Play requires apps to target a recent API level to stay listed.</li><li><strong>Play Services</strong> add maps, sign-in and payments beyond the base OS.</li><li><strong>Firebase</strong> provides database, auth and storage without your own server.</li><li>Not every device has Play Services &mdash; some markets use alternative stores.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Setting <em>minSdk</em> to a recent version is the easiest way to lock out exactly the users you most want to reach. Checking what devices your audience actually carries comes before choosing it.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Every technical decision in this course &mdash; layout choices, storage, network handling &mdash; is shaped by which devices you decided to support.</p></div>",
  "Installing Android Studio": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Installing Android Studio</strong><br><span>One download, a long first run, and then you are set up for good.</span></div><p>Download Android Studio from developer.android.com. The installer bundles the IDE, the Android SDK and an emulator image. The first launch downloads several gigabytes, so start it when you have the bandwidth.</p><p>It is demanding on hardware. 8 GB of RAM makes it comfortable; 4 GB works if you use a physical phone instead of the emulator, which is often the better choice on a modest machine anyway.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Download only from <strong>developer.android.com</strong>.</li><li>First run fetches the SDK and an emulator image &mdash; several gigabytes.</li><li>8 GB RAM is comfortable; 4 GB works if you test on a real phone.</li><li>The emulator needs hardware virtualisation enabled in the BIOS.</li><li>Keep the SDK path free of spaces and non-English characters.</li><li>A physical Android phone over USB is faster and lighter than the emulator.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">On a 4 GB laptop, skip the emulator entirely: enable Developer Options on your own phone, connect it by USB, and run the app there. It is faster, uses no extra RAM, and shows you real device behaviour.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Getting the environment right once saves hours later. Most beginner problems in Module 3 trace back to an incomplete or interrupted first-run download.</p></div>",
  "Setting Up the Development Environment": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Setting Up the Development Environment</strong><br><span>SDK components, a device to run on, and a project that builds.</span></div><p>After installation, use the <strong>SDK Manager</strong> to confirm you have an SDK platform and the build tools, and the <strong>Device Manager</strong> to create an emulator or connect a phone.</p><p>To use a physical device: enable <em>Developer Options</em> by tapping Build Number seven times in Settings, then turn on <em>USB debugging</em> and accept the authorisation prompt when you connect.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>SDK Manager</strong> installs platforms and build tools.</li><li><strong>Device Manager</strong> creates emulators (AVDs).</li><li>Physical device: tap Build Number 7 times, then enable USB debugging.</li><li>Accept the USB debugging authorisation prompt on the phone itself.</li><li>Gradle sync must complete before anything will build.</li><li>Work offline where possible &mdash; Gradle caches dependencies after the first build.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">The first Gradle sync downloads dependencies and can take a long time on a slow connection. It is a one-off per project type; afterwards the cache makes builds much faster.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>A working device and a green Gradle sync are the two things to confirm before writing any code. Almost every &ldquo;my app will not run&rdquo; question comes down to one of them.</p></div>",
  "Course Roadmap": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Course Roadmap</strong><br><span>What you will build, in what order, and why that order.</span></div><p>The course moves from language, to tools, to interface, to data, to publishing. Each stage assumes the last, which is why working out of order causes trouble.</p><p>Modules 2&ndash;3 are Kotlin and Android Studio. Modules 4&ndash;6 are the interface and navigation. Modules 7&ndash;9 are data: storage, Firebase and networking. Modules 10&ndash;14 add device features, design, security, testing and publishing. Modules 15&ndash;18 cover AI tooling, freelancing and the portfolio projects.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Language and tools first &mdash; everything else depends on them.</li><li>Interface and navigation before data, so you have somewhere to display it.</li><li>Storage, then Firebase, then networking &mdash; local before remote.</li><li>Security, testing and publishing before the portfolio projects.</li><li>Modules 17 and 18 build eleven apps that reuse everything prior.</li><li>Do the quizzes; they gate the next lesson deliberately.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Learners who skip Module 2 to reach the interface faster usually return to it by Module 5, because layout code is Kotlin code. The order exists for that reason.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Knowing the shape of the course helps you judge progress and lets you tell a client what you can already do &mdash; useful well before you finish.</p></div>",
  "Introduction to Kotlin": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Introduction to Kotlin</strong><br><span>Concise, safe, and designed to remove the mistakes Java made easy.</span></div><p>Kotlin is a statically typed language that runs on the Java Virtual Machine and interoperates fully with Java. Google made it the preferred Android language because it is shorter to write and removes whole categories of bug.</p><p>The headline feature is <strong>null safety</strong>: the type system distinguishes values that can be null from those that cannot, so the null pointer exception that dominates Java crash reports is caught at compile time instead.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Statically typed, JVM-based, fully interoperable with Java.</li><li><strong>Null safety</strong> is built into the type system.</li><li>Type inference means you rarely write the type twice.</li><li>Semicolons are optional and conventionally omitted.</li><li>Far less boilerplate: a data class replaces dozens of Java lines.</li><li>Everything in this course is Kotlin, but you will read Java in older projects.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">In Kotlin, <em>var name: String</em> can never hold null; <em>var name: String?</em> can. The compiler forces you to handle the second case before you can use it, which is where the crash used to happen.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Null safety alone removes the single most common cause of Android app crashes, which is why the language change was worth making.</p></div>",
  "Variables and Data Types": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Variables and Data Types</strong><br><span>val for things that will not change, var for things that will.</span></div><p><strong>val</strong> declares a read-only reference; <strong>var</strong> declares one you can reassign. Prefer <em>val</em> by default &mdash; it makes intent clear and prevents accidental modification.</p><p>Kotlin infers types, so <em>val count = 5</em> is an Int without saying so. Declare the type explicitly when it aids readability or when the value comes later.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>val</strong> = read-only; <strong>var</strong> = reassignable. Default to <em>val</em>.</li><li>Types: <em>Int</em>, <em>Long</em>, <em>Double</em>, <em>Float</em>, <em>Boolean</em>, <em>Char</em>, <em>String</em>.</li><li>Type inference: <em>val n = 5</em> is an Int without annotation.</li><li><em>val</em> means the reference cannot change, not that a list's contents cannot.</li><li>String templates: <em>&quot;Hello, $name&quot;</em> inserts a value directly.</li><li>Kotlin does not implicitly convert numeric types &mdash; use <em>toInt()</em>, <em>toDouble()</em>.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>val list = mutableListOf(1, 2)</em> then <em>list.add(3)</em> is legal. <em>val</em> prevents reassigning <em>list</em> to a different list; it does not freeze the contents. That distinction catches many beginners.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Choosing <em>val</em> wherever possible is the cheapest way to make Android code easier to reason about, particularly once several screens share state.</p></div>",
  "Operators": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Operators</strong><br><span>Arithmetic, comparison, logic &mdash; plus a few Kotlin adds.</span></div><p>Kotlin has the usual arithmetic (<em>+ &minus; * / %</em>), comparison (<em>== != &lt; &gt; &lt;= &gt;=</em>) and logical (<em>&amp;&amp; || !</em>) operators.</p><p>Two are worth singling out. <strong>==</strong> compares values in Kotlin (unlike Java, where it compares references), and <strong>?:</strong>, the Elvis operator, supplies a default when the left side is null.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><em>==</em> compares <strong>values</strong>; <em>===</em> compares references.</li><li>Integer division truncates: <em>7 / 2</em> is 3, not 3.5.</li><li><em>%</em> gives the remainder: <em>7 % 2</em> is 1.</li><li><strong>Elvis</strong> <em>?:</em> supplies a fallback: <em>name ?: &quot;Guest&quot;</em>.</li><li>Safe call <em>?.</em> returns null instead of crashing on a null receiver.</li><li><em>&amp;&amp;</em> and <em>||</em> short-circuit &mdash; the right side may not run.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>val display = username ?: &quot;Guest&quot;</em> gives a usable value whether or not <em>username</em> is null. Combined with <em>?.</em> it removes most of the defensive null checking Java required.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>The safe call and Elvis operators together are how idiomatic Kotlin handles absent data, and you will use them constantly once network responses appear in Module 9.</p></div>",
  "User Input": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 User Input</strong><br><span>Reading what the user typed &mdash; and never trusting it.</span></div><p>In a console exercise you read input with <em>readLine()</em>. In an Android app you read it from an <em>EditText</em> via its <em>text</em> property, then convert it to the type you need.</p><p>Conversion is where apps crash. <em>toInt()</em> throws if the text is not a number; <em>toIntOrNull()</em> returns null instead, which you can handle. Always use the safe variant on user input.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Console: <em>readLine()</em>. Android: the <em>EditText</em>'s <em>text</em> property.</li><li>Input arrives as a <strong>String</strong> and must be converted deliberately.</li><li>Use <em>toIntOrNull()</em>, not <em>toInt()</em> &mdash; it returns null rather than crashing.</li><li>Check for empty input before converting.</li><li>Set <em>inputType</em> on the field to bring up the right keyboard.</li><li>Validate and show a clear message; never fail silently.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>val age = input.toIntOrNull()</em> then <em>if (age == null) showError()</em> handles a user typing &ldquo;twenty&rdquo; gracefully. Using <em>toInt()</em> instead closes the app.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Unhandled input conversion is one of the most common causes of crashes in beginner apps, and the safe variants remove it entirely.</p></div>",
  "Conditional Statements": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Conditional Statements</strong><br><span>if, else and when &mdash; and in Kotlin they return values.</span></div><p><em>if / else</em> works as expected, but in Kotlin it is an <strong>expression</strong>: it produces a value you can assign. <em>when</em> replaces Java's switch and handles ranges, types and arbitrary conditions.</p><p><em>when</em> is the idiomatic choice for more than two branches. It reads more clearly than a chain of else-ifs and the compiler can check that an enum is handled exhaustively.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><em>if</em> is an expression: <em>val max = if (a &gt; b) a else b</em>.</li><li><em>when</em> replaces switch and handles multiple branch types.</li><li><em>when</em> matches ranges (<em>in 1..9</em>), types (<em>is String</em>) and conditions.</li><li><em>else</em> is required when <em>when</em> is used as an expression.</li><li>Prefer <em>when</em> over three or more else-if branches.</li><li>No fall-through: only the matching branch runs.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>val grade = when (score) { in 80..100 -&gt; &quot;A&quot;; in 70..79 -&gt; &quot;B&quot;; else -&gt; &quot;F&quot; }</em> assigns directly. The equivalent if-chain is longer and easier to get wrong.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Because both forms return values, Kotlin code tends to assign once rather than declaring a variable and mutating it, which is easier to follow.</p></div>",
  "Loops": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Loops</strong><br><span>Repeat over a range, over a collection, or until a condition changes.</span></div><p><em>for</em> iterates over ranges and collections. <em>while</em> repeats while a condition holds. <em>do-while</em> runs at least once before testing.</p><p>Kotlin's ranges are expressive: <em>1..5</em> counts up, <em>5 downTo 1</em> counts down, <em>1..10 step 2</em> skips, and <em>until</em> excludes the upper bound.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><em>for (i in 1..5)</em> includes 5; <em>for (i in 1 until 5)</em> stops at 4.</li><li><em>downTo</em> counts backwards; <em>step</em> changes the increment.</li><li>Iterate a collection directly: <em>for (item in list)</em>.</li><li><em>forEach</em> is the functional equivalent and reads well for short bodies.</li><li><em>break</em> exits the loop; <em>continue</em> skips to the next iteration.</li><li>Avoid modifying a collection while iterating over it.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>for (i in 1 until list.size)</em> is safe for index access; <em>1..list.size</em> would run one past the end and crash. The <em>until</em> form exists precisely to avoid that off-by-one.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Off-by-one errors on list indices are among the most common runtime crashes, and Kotlin's range syntax makes the correct version the shorter one.</p></div>",
  "Functions": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Functions</strong><br><span>Name a block of work, give it inputs, get a result.</span></div><p>A function is declared with <em>fun</em>, takes typed parameters, and declares a return type. If it returns nothing the type is <em>Unit</em> and can be omitted.</p><p>Kotlin adds two conveniences worth using: <strong>default parameter values</strong>, which remove the need for overloads, and <strong>named arguments</strong>, which make calls with several parameters readable.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><em>fun add(a: Int, b: Int): Int { return a + b }</em>.</li><li>Single-expression form: <em>fun add(a: Int, b: Int) = a + b</em>.</li><li><strong>Default values</strong>: <em>fun greet(name: String = &quot;there&quot;)</em>.</li><li><strong>Named arguments</strong>: <em>greet(name = &quot;Musu&quot;)</em>.</li><li>Return type <em>Unit</em> means it returns nothing and may be omitted.</li><li>Keep functions short and single-purpose; they are easier to test.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>fun createUser(name: String, isAdmin: Boolean = false, sendEmail: Boolean = true)</em> called as <em>createUser(&quot;Musu&quot;, sendEmail = false)</em> is clear at the call site. Three boolean positional arguments would not be.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Named arguments and defaults remove most of the overloading and builder patterns Java needed, which keeps Android code considerably shorter.</p></div>",
  "Arrays": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Arrays</strong><br><span>A fixed-size, indexed sequence of values.</span></div><p>An <em>Array</em> holds a fixed number of elements of one type, accessed by a zero-based index. Its size is set at creation and cannot change.</p><p>In practice Android code uses <em>List</em> far more often, because most data has a size that varies. Arrays remain useful for fixed collections and when interoperating with Java APIs that expect them.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Zero-based indexing: the first element is <em>arr[0]</em>.</li><li>Size is fixed at creation; <em>arr.size</em> reports it.</li><li><em>arrayOf(1, 2, 3)</em> creates one; <em>IntArray(5)</em> creates a sized one.</li><li>Accessing an out-of-range index throws an exception.</li><li>Prefer <em>List</em> when the number of items can change.</li><li><em>arr.indices</em> gives a safe range for iteration.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>arr[arr.size]</em> is always out of bounds, because the last valid index is <em>size - 1</em>. Iterating with <em>for (i in arr.indices)</em> avoids the mistake entirely.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Index errors are a frequent source of crashes; using <em>indices</em> or iterating the elements directly removes the arithmetic that causes them.</p></div>",
  "Lists": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Lists</strong><br><span>The collection Android code actually uses.</span></div><p><em>listOf()</em> creates a read-only list; <em>mutableListOf()</em> creates one you can add to and remove from. Most app data &mdash; contacts, messages, products &mdash; is a list.</p><p>Kotlin's collection functions do most of the work you would otherwise loop for: <em>filter</em>, <em>map</em>, <em>sortedBy</em>, <em>first</em>, <em>any</em>, <em>sumOf</em>.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><em>listOf()</em> read-only; <em>mutableListOf()</em> supports add and remove.</li><li><em>filter { }</em> selects; <em>map { }</em> transforms.</li><li><em>sortedBy { }</em> orders; <em>find { }</em> returns the first match or null.</li><li><em>isEmpty()</em> and <em>isNotEmpty()</em> are clearer than checking size.</li><li><em>list[0]</em> on an empty list crashes; <em>firstOrNull()</em> does not.</li><li>Lists feed the RecyclerView in Module 4 &mdash; the standard way to display them.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>products.filter { it.price &lt; 100 }.sortedBy { it.name }</em> replaces a loop, a temporary list and a comparator in one readable line.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Almost every screen you build displays a list. These functions are what turn raw data into what the screen should show.</p></div>",
  "Object-Oriented Programming Basics": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Object-Oriented Programming Basics</strong><br><span>Bundle data with the behaviour that belongs to it.</span></div><p>Object-oriented programming organises code into <strong>classes</strong> that hold data (properties) and the operations on it (methods). An <strong>object</strong> is one instance of a class.</p><p>Android is built this way: an Activity is a class, a View is a class, your data model is a class. Understanding the four ideas &mdash; encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism, abstraction &mdash; is what makes the framework legible.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Class</strong> = blueprint; <strong>object</strong> = one instance of it.</li><li><strong>Encapsulation</strong>: keep data private, expose controlled access.</li><li><strong>Inheritance</strong>: a class extends another and reuses its behaviour.</li><li><strong>Polymorphism</strong>: different classes respond to the same call differently.</li><li><strong>Abstraction</strong>: expose what something does, hide how.</li><li>Kotlin classes are <em>final</em> by default; mark them <em>open</em> to allow inheritance.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>class MainActivity : AppCompatActivity()</em> is inheritance in the very first line of every Android screen you write. Your class gets the whole lifecycle from the parent and overrides only what it needs.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>You cannot read Android's own code without this vocabulary, and every screen, view and adapter in the rest of the course is an application of it.</p></div>",
  "Classes and Objects": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Classes and Objects</strong><br><span>Constructors, properties, and the data class that saves you fifty lines.</span></div><p>A Kotlin class declares its constructor parameters in the header: <em>class User(val name: String, var age: Int)</em>. That single line creates the properties and the constructor together.</p><p>For classes that only carry data, <strong>data class</strong> additionally generates <em>equals</em>, <em>hashCode</em>, <em>toString</em> and <em>copy</em>. Most model classes in an Android app should be data classes.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Primary constructor sits in the class header.</li><li><em>val</em> parameters become read-only properties; <em>var</em> ones are mutable.</li><li><strong>data class</strong> generates equals, hashCode, toString and copy.</li><li><em>init { }</em> blocks run when an instance is created.</li><li>Create an instance without <em>new</em>: <em>val u = User(&quot;Musu&quot;, 30)</em>.</li><li><em>copy()</em> makes a modified duplicate: <em>u.copy(age = 31)</em>.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>data class Product(val id: Int, val name: String, val price: Double)</em> is a complete model class. Comparing two products, printing one, or copying one with a changed price all work without another line of code.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Data classes are what make Kotlin model layers so short, and they are the shape everything in Modules 7 to 9 stores, sends and receives.</p></div>",
  "M2:Practice Exercises": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 M2:Practice Exercises</strong><br><span>Kotlin practice: types, control flow, functions, collections, classes.</span></div><p>This lesson consolidates Module 2. Work each exercise in Android Studio's Kotlin playground or a scratch file, and run it &mdash; reading code teaches far less than watching it fail and fixing it.</p><p>Aim to write, not to recognise. If you can only follow an example when it is in front of you, you have not learned it yet.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Types: convert user input safely with <em>toIntOrNull()</em>.</li><li>Control flow: rewrite an if-chain of three or more branches as <em>when</em>.</li><li>Loops: use <em>indices</em> or iterate elements directly rather than computing bounds.</li><li>Functions: extract any repeated block into a named function.</li><li>Collections: replace a loop with <em>filter</em>, <em>map</em> or <em>sortedBy</em>.</li><li>Classes: model your data as a <em>data class</em> before writing UI code.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A good self-test: write a small program that reads a list of student names and scores, filters those above a threshold, sorts them, and prints them. It exercises every topic in the module in about twenty lines.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Everything from Module 4 onward assumes this material. Time spent here is recovered several times over once you start wiring layouts to data.</p></div>",
  "Android Studio Interface": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Android Studio Interface</strong><br><span>Four panels do almost everything.</span></div><p>The <strong>Project</strong> panel shows your files; the <strong>editor</strong> holds your code; <strong>Logcat</strong> shows what the running app reports; and the <strong>toolbar</strong> selects a device and runs the app.</p><p>Two habits pay off immediately: learn the Run and Debug buttons, and keep Logcat open. Almost every question about why an app misbehaves is answered there.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><strong>Project</strong> panel &mdash; files, switchable between Android and Project views.</li><li><strong>Editor</strong> &mdash; code, with red underlines for compile errors.</li><li><strong>Logcat</strong> &mdash; runtime output and crash stack traces.</li><li><strong>Build</strong> panel &mdash; compile errors and Gradle output.</li><li>Run (green triangle) installs and starts; Debug attaches the debugger.</li><li><em>Ctrl+Shift+F</em> / <em>Cmd+Shift+F</em> searches the whole project.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A red underline in the editor is a <em>compile</em> error &mdash; the app will not build. A crash after the app starts is a <em>runtime</em> error, and its explanation is in Logcat, not the editor.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Distinguishing compile errors from runtime crashes is the first debugging skill, and it determines which panel to look at.</p></div>",
  "Project Structure": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Project Structure</strong><br><span>Where each kind of file belongs, and why it matters.</span></div><p>An Android project separates code from resources. Kotlin lives under <em>app/src/main/java</em>; layouts, images and strings live under <em>app/src/main/res</em>; and <em>AndroidManifest.xml</em> declares the app's components and permissions.</p><p>The <em>res</em> folder's subdirectory names are meaningful. <em>layout</em>, <em>drawable</em>, <em>values</em> and <em>mipmap</em> each have a defined purpose, and Android selects among qualified variants automatically by device.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><em>java/</em> &mdash; Kotlin and Java source (the folder name is historical).</li><li><em>res/layout/</em> &mdash; XML layouts. <em>res/drawable/</em> &mdash; images and shapes.</li><li><em>res/values/</em> &mdash; strings, colours, dimensions, styles.</li><li><em>res/mipmap/</em> &mdash; launcher icons only.</li><li><em>AndroidManifest.xml</em> &mdash; components, permissions, app metadata.</li><li><em>build.gradle</em> &mdash; dependencies and build configuration.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Putting user-visible text in <em>res/values/strings.xml</em> rather than hard-coding it means translating the app later is a matter of adding one file, not editing every layout.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>The structure is a convention the build system relies on. Files in the wrong folder either fail to build or silently fail to be found at runtime.</p></div>",
  "Emulator Setup": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Emulator Setup</strong><br><span>A virtual device for testing, and when to use a real one instead.</span></div><p>The <strong>Device Manager</strong> creates an Android Virtual Device: choose a phone profile, a system image (an API level), and start it. It behaves like a real device for most purposes.</p><p>It is heavy. On a machine with limited RAM, a physical phone connected over USB is faster, uses no extra memory, and shows you genuine performance &mdash; which matters when your users are on modest hardware.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Create an AVD in <strong>Device Manager</strong>: profile + system image.</li><li>Choose an API level matching the devices you intend to support.</li><li>The emulator needs hardware virtualisation and a lot of RAM.</li><li>A physical device over USB is faster and more representative.</li><li>The emulator can simulate location, network speed and incoming calls.</li><li>Test on at least one low-end configuration, not only a flagship profile.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">The emulator lets you throttle the network to simulate a slow connection. Testing your app that way, rather than on fast Wi-Fi, reveals loading states you would otherwise never see.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Whichever you use, testing on something resembling your users' actual devices is what stops an app that feels fine in development from being unusable in the field.</p></div>",
  "Gradle Basics": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Gradle Basics</strong><br><span>The build system that turns your source into an installable app.</span></div><p>Gradle compiles your code, merges resources, downloads dependencies and packages everything into an APK or AAB. You configure it in <em>build.gradle</em> files: one for the project, one for each module.</p><p>The module-level file is the one you edit most. It declares <em>minSdk</em>, <em>targetSdk</em>, the version name, and the <em>dependencies</em> block where every library you add appears as one line.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Project-level <em>build.gradle</em> configures plugins and repositories.</li><li>Module-level <em>build.gradle</em> holds SDK versions and dependencies.</li><li>Adding a library means one line in <em>dependencies</em> plus a Gradle sync.</li><li><em>versionCode</em> is an integer Play uses; <em>versionName</em> is what users see.</li><li>Gradle caches dependencies, so only the first build is slow.</li><li>A failed sync blocks everything &mdash; read the Build panel message.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Adding <em>implementation(&quot;com.squareup.retrofit2:retrofit:2.9.0&quot;)</em> and syncing is the whole process for pulling in the networking library used in Module 9.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Almost every capability you add to an app in this course begins with a dependency line, so being comfortable in this file matters more than it first appears.</p></div>",
  "Android Manifest": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Android Manifest</strong><br><span>The declaration file Android reads before your code runs.</span></div><p><em>AndroidManifest.xml</em> tells the system what your app contains: its activities, its permissions, its launcher icon, and which screen starts first.</p><p>Anything not declared here does not exist as far as Android is concerned. An activity missing from the manifest crashes the app when you try to open it, with an error that names the class.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Every activity must be declared, or launching it crashes the app.</li><li>The <em>LAUNCHER</em> intent filter marks the screen that opens first.</li><li>Permissions are declared here: <em>INTERNET</em>, <em>CAMERA</em>, <em>ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION</em>.</li><li>Declaring a dangerous permission is not enough &mdash; you must also request it at runtime.</li><li>App name, icon and theme are set on the <em>&lt;application&gt;</em> element.</li><li>Request only the permissions you genuinely use; Play review checks this.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Forgetting <em>&lt;uses-permission android:name=&quot;android.permission.INTERNET&quot;/&gt;</em> makes every network call fail with a security exception, which looks like a networking bug and is a manifest one.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>The manifest is the first place to check when something works in code but fails at runtime, particularly around permissions and missing activities.</p></div>",
  "Logcat": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Logcat</strong><br><span>The running app talking to you.</span></div><p>Logcat streams messages from the device: your own log statements, framework messages, and the stack trace when something crashes. It is the primary debugging tool.</p><p>Log with the level that matches the message: <em>Log.d</em> for debugging, <em>Log.i</em> for information, <em>Log.w</em> for warnings, <em>Log.e</em> for errors. Filter by your tag or by package to cut the noise.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><em>Log.d(TAG, &quot;message&quot;)</em> writes a debug line.</li><li>Levels: Verbose, Debug, Info, Warn, Error, Assert.</li><li>Filter by package or tag &mdash; unfiltered Logcat is unreadable.</li><li>A crash produces a stack trace; read from the top for the exception type.</li><li>&ldquo;Caused by&rdquo; lines point to the real origin.</li><li>Never log passwords, tokens or personal data &mdash; logs are readable on device.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A crash trace beginning <em>java.lang.NullPointerException</em> with a &ldquo;Caused by&rdquo; line naming your file and line number tells you exactly where to look. The first line names the problem; the caused-by names the place.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Reading a stack trace is the single most useful debugging skill in this course, and it is mostly a matter of knowing which line to read first.</p></div>",
  "Debugging Basics": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Debugging Basics</strong><br><span>Stop the app where it matters and look at the values.</span></div><p>A <strong>breakpoint</strong> pauses execution at a chosen line so you can inspect variables. Set one by clicking the gutter, then run with Debug rather than Run.</p><p>When paused, <em>step over</em> executes the current line, <em>step into</em> enters a function call, and the Variables panel shows every value in scope. This beats adding log statements and rebuilding.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Click the gutter to set a breakpoint; run with <strong>Debug</strong>.</li><li><strong>Step over</strong> runs the line; <strong>step into</strong> enters the call.</li><li>The Variables panel shows live values at the pause point.</li><li>Conditional breakpoints pause only when an expression is true.</li><li>Debugging beats log-and-rebuild for anything non-trivial.</li><li>Reproduce the bug reliably before you start debugging it.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">For a crash that only happens on the fiftieth item, a conditional breakpoint on <em>index == 49</em> pauses exactly there instead of making you step through forty-nine iterations.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Being able to inspect state at the moment of failure is what turns guesswork into diagnosis, and it is the skill Module 13 builds on.</p></div>",
  "Project Organization": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Project Organization</strong><br><span>Group files so the next person &mdash; usually you &mdash; can find them.</span></div><p>As an app grows past a handful of files, put related classes into packages: <em>ui</em>, <em>data</em>, <em>model</em>, <em>network</em>, <em>util</em>. The alternative is one flat folder of forty files.</p><p>Naming conventions matter for the same reason. Activities end in <em>Activity</em>, layouts are named after what they lay out, and resource ids describe the element rather than its position.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Package by role: <em>ui</em>, <em>data</em>, <em>model</em>, <em>network</em>, <em>util</em>.</li><li>Classes are <em>PascalCase</em>; functions and variables are <em>camelCase</em>.</li><li>Layout files are lowercase with underscores: <em>activity_main.xml</em>.</li><li>Resource ids describe purpose: <em>btnSubmit</em>, not <em>button2</em>.</li><li>Keep one class per file, named after the class.</li><li>Delete unused files; dead code is read as if it were live.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>button2</em> tells you nothing six weeks later, and nothing at all to a client's next developer. <em>btnSubmitOrder</em> costs the same to type and explains itself.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Freelance work in Module 16 often means reading someone else's project. Organisation is what determines whether that takes an hour or a week &mdash; and yours will be read the same way.</p></div>",
  "Best Practices": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Best Practices</strong><br><span>Habits that keep an app maintainable as it grows.</span></div><p>Most of what makes Android code good is unglamorous: keep strings in resources, keep functions short, handle errors, avoid doing work on the main thread, and do not repeat yourself.</p><p>The main-thread rule is the one with visible consequences. Any slow work &mdash; network calls, database queries, file reads &mdash; must run off the UI thread, or the app freezes and Android may kill it.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Never do network or disk work on the <strong>main thread</strong>.</li><li>Keep user-visible text in <em>strings.xml</em>.</li><li>Handle errors and show the user something useful.</li><li>Extract repeated code into functions; do not copy and paste.</li><li>Prefer <em>val</em> over <em>var</em>, and small functions over large ones.</li><li>Test on a low-end device and a slow connection before release.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">An app that freezes for four seconds during a network call is not slow, it is broken &mdash; Android shows an &ldquo;application not responding&rdquo; dialog and the user force-closes it. Module 9 covers doing this properly.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>These practices are what separate an app that survives its second month from one that becomes unmaintainable, and they are what a client's reviewer looks for.</p></div>",
  "XML Layouts": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 XML Layouts</strong><br><span>The screen described as a tree of views.</span></div><p>An Android screen is defined in XML: a root <strong>ViewGroup</strong> containing <strong>Views</strong>. Each element has attributes for size, position, appearance and an <em>id</em> your Kotlin code uses to find it.</p><p>Every view needs <em>layout_width</em> and <em>layout_height</em>. The three values are <em>match_parent</em> (fill the parent), <em>wrap_content</em> (fit the content), or a fixed size in <em>dp</em>.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>A layout is a tree: a ViewGroup containing Views.</li><li><em>layout_width</em> and <em>layout_height</em> are required on every view.</li><li><em>match_parent</em> fills, <em>wrap_content</em> fits, <em>dp</em> fixes.</li><li>Use <strong>dp</strong> for sizes and <strong>sp</strong> for text, never raw pixels.</li><li><em>android:id=&quot;@+id/name&quot;</em> creates an id your code can find.</li><li>The Design and Split views help, but read the XML &mdash; it is the truth.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>dp</em> scales with screen density, so a 48dp button is physically similar on a cheap phone and an expensive one. A size in pixels would be tiny on a high-density screen.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Every screen in the rest of this course is an XML layout, and using dp and sp correctly is what makes an app usable across the range of devices your users actually have.</p></div>",
  "TextView": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 TextView</strong><br><span>Display text &mdash; the most used view in Android.</span></div><p>A <em>TextView</em> shows text the user cannot edit. Its key attributes are <em>text</em>, <em>textSize</em>, <em>textColor</em>, <em>textStyle</em> and <em>gravity</em>.</p><p>Set the text from Kotlin with <em>textView.text = &quot;...&quot;</em>. Reference strings from resources rather than hard-coding them, so the app can be translated.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><em>android:text=&quot;@string/greeting&quot;</em> pulls from strings.xml.</li><li>In Kotlin: <em>binding.tvGreeting.text = &quot;Hello&quot;</em>.</li><li><em>textSize</em> in <strong>sp</strong>; <em>textColor</em> from a colour resource.</li><li><em>maxLines</em> with <em>ellipsize</em> truncates long text cleanly.</li><li><em>gravity</em> positions text inside the view; <em>layout_gravity</em> positions the view.</li><li>Long text without <em>maxLines</em> will push the rest of your layout off screen.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A product name of unknown length in a list item needs <em>android:maxLines=&quot;1&quot;</em> and <em>android:ellipsize=&quot;end&quot;</em>, or one long name breaks the alignment of every row.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Text that varies in length is the commonest cause of layouts that look right in the editor and wrong with real data.</p></div>",
  "EditText": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 EditText</strong><br><span>Where the user types, and where validation begins.</span></div><p>An <em>EditText</em> accepts typed input. Set <em>hint</em> rather than <em>text</em> so the field shows guidance that disappears when typing starts, and set <em>inputType</em> so the right keyboard appears.</p><p>Read it with <em>editText.text.toString()</em>. The value is always a String, so convert deliberately &mdash; and safely &mdash; before using it as a number.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><em>hint</em> shows guidance; <em>text</em> pre-fills the field.</li><li><em>inputType</em>: <em>text</em>, <em>number</em>, <em>textEmailAddress</em>, <em>textPassword</em>.</li><li>Read with <em>.text.toString()</em>, then <em>trim()</em>.</li><li>Convert with <em>toIntOrNull()</em>, never bare <em>toInt()</em>.</li><li>Set <em>error</em> on the field to show inline validation messages.</li><li>Never store a password in plain text or log its value.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Setting <em>inputType=&quot;number&quot;</em> gives a numeric keyboard, which reduces mistyping. It does not prevent invalid input &mdash; the user can still paste text &mdash; so server-side or code validation is still required.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Input validation in the next lesson depends entirely on reading and converting these values safely.</p></div>",
  "Button": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 Button</strong><br><span>The control that makes something happen.</span></div><p>A <em>Button</em> is a clickable view. Give it an <em>id</em>, set its <em>text</em>, and attach a click listener in Kotlin with <em>setOnClickListener { }</em>.</p><p>Keep the listener short. It should read the input, call a function that does the work, and update the interface &mdash; not contain the logic itself, which is far harder to test.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><em>btn.setOnClickListener { doThing() }</em> attaches the handler.</li><li>Give buttons a minimum touch target of <strong>48dp</strong>.</li><li>Disable the button while a slow operation runs, to prevent double submission.</li><li>Label the action, not the mechanism: <em>Save order</em>, not <em>Submit</em>.</li><li>Keep listener bodies short; delegate to named functions.</li><li><em>isEnabled</em> and <em>visibility</em> control availability and presence separately.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A submit button left enabled during a network call gets tapped three times on a slow connection, sending three orders. Disabling it on the first tap and re-enabling on completion prevents that entirely.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Double submission is one of the most common real bugs in field-tested apps, and it appears precisely on the slow connections your users have.</p></div>",
  "ImageView": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 ImageView</strong><br><span>Show a picture, and keep it from destroying your memory budget.</span></div><p>An <em>ImageView</em> displays an image from resources (<em>@drawable/logo</em>), from a file, or from the network. <em>scaleType</em> controls how the image fits the view.</p><p>Large images are a leading cause of crashes. A photo straight from a camera can be several megapixels; loading it at full size into a small view exhausts memory. Use an image library, which handles resizing and caching for you.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><em>scaleType</em>: <em>centerCrop</em> fills and crops, <em>fitCenter</em> fits inside.</li><li><em>src</em> sets the image; <em>contentDescription</em> is required for accessibility.</li><li>Loading a full-resolution photo into a small view can crash the app.</li><li>Use a library (Glide, Coil) for network images &mdash; it resizes and caches.</li><li>Provide a placeholder and an error image for network loads.</li><li>Keep bundled images modest; they inflate the app's download size.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\"><em>Coil</em> in one line &mdash; <em>imageView.load(url)</em> &mdash; handles downloading, resizing, caching and cancellation. Doing it by hand correctly is considerably more code and usually gets the memory part wrong.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Image handling is where apps most often crash on low-memory devices, which is exactly the hardware much of the audience for these apps is using.</p></div>",
  "RecyclerView": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 RecyclerView</strong><br><span>Show a long list without holding every row in memory.</span></div><p>A <em>RecyclerView</em> displays a scrolling list by <strong>recycling</strong> a small number of row views as the user scrolls. A thousand-item list keeps only the visible rows plus a few, rather than a thousand views.</p><p>Three pieces are required: a <strong>layout</strong> for one row, an <strong>Adapter</strong> that binds data to that row, and a <strong>LayoutManager</strong> that decides the arrangement.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Needs a row layout, an <strong>Adapter</strong> and a <strong>LayoutManager</strong>.</li><li><em>ViewHolder</em> caches the row's view references so lookup happens once.</li><li><em>onCreateViewHolder</em> inflates a row; <em>onBindViewHolder</em> fills it with data.</li><li><em>getItemCount()</em> must return the data size, or rows go missing.</li><li>Call <em>notifyDataSetChanged()</em> &mdash; or better, DiffUtil &mdash; after data changes.</li><li>Never do slow work inside <em>onBindViewHolder</em>; it runs during scrolling.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A list that stutters while scrolling almost always has slow work in <em>onBindViewHolder</em> &mdash; loading an image synchronously, formatting a date, or querying a database. That method runs for every row that scrolls into view.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>RecyclerView appears in nearly every app you will build from here on, and it is the single most asked-about component in Android interviews.</p></div>",
  "ScrollView": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 ScrollView</strong><br><span>Let content taller than the screen be reached.</span></div><p>A <em>ScrollView</em> makes its contents scrollable vertically. It takes exactly one direct child, so wrap everything in a LinearLayout or ConstraintLayout first.</p><p>Never put a RecyclerView inside a ScrollView. Both scroll, and nesting them destroys RecyclerView's recycling &mdash; every row is created at once, which is the problem RecyclerView exists to solve.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>A ScrollView takes exactly <strong>one</strong> direct child.</li><li>Wrap multiple views in a LinearLayout inside it.</li><li><em>HorizontalScrollView</em> scrolls sideways.</li><li>Never nest a RecyclerView inside a ScrollView.</li><li>Use <em>NestedScrollView</em> when working with Material scrolling behaviours.</li><li>Long forms need one; on a small screen the last field is otherwise unreachable.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A registration form with eight fields fits on a large phone and not on a small one. Without a ScrollView the submit button is simply unreachable for those users, and the form cannot be completed at all.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>This is a common accessibility failure that never shows up in development if you only test on one large device.</p></div>",
  "ConstraintLayout": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 ConstraintLayout</strong><br><span>Position views by relationship, in one flat layout.</span></div><p><em>ConstraintLayout</em> positions each view by constraining its edges to the parent or to other views. It replaces deeply nested layouts with a single flat one, which is both easier to read and faster to draw.</p><p>Every view needs at least one horizontal and one vertical constraint. A view without them compiles and then renders at the top-left corner at runtime, which is the most common ConstraintLayout surprise.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Constrain each view horizontally <em>and</em> vertically, or it lands at 0,0.</li><li>Constrain to the parent or to another view's edge.</li><li><em>0dp</em> width with both side constraints means &ldquo;match constraints&rdquo;.</li><li><strong>Guidelines</strong> position at a percentage; <strong>chains</strong> distribute a group.</li><li><strong>Barriers</strong> track the largest of several views.</li><li>Flat beats nested: fewer levels means faster layout passes.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A view that looks correct in the Design editor but appears at the top-left when you run the app is missing a constraint. The editor shows your intent; the device shows the constraints you actually declared.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>ConstraintLayout is the default for new Android layouts, and the missing-constraint symptom is worth recognising immediately because it is so common.</p></div>",
  "LinearLayout": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 LinearLayout</strong><br><span>Stack views in a row or a column.</span></div><p>A <em>LinearLayout</em> arranges children in a single direction, set by <em>orientation</em>: <em>vertical</em> or <em>horizontal</em>. It is the simplest layout and remains useful for genuinely linear arrangements.</p><p><em>layout_weight</em> distributes leftover space proportionally. Two children with weight 1 each take half the remaining space, which is how you split a row evenly without hard-coding widths.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><em>orientation</em> is required: <em>vertical</em> or <em>horizontal</em>.</li><li><em>layout_weight</em> divides remaining space proportionally.</li><li>Set the dimension along the axis to <em>0dp</em> when using weight.</li><li>Simple and readable, but nesting several is slow &mdash; prefer ConstraintLayout then.</li><li><em>gravity</em> aligns children; <em>layout_gravity</em> aligns this view in its parent.</li><li>Good for toolbars, form rows and short vertical stacks.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Two buttons side by side, each half the width: put them in a horizontal LinearLayout with <em>layout_width=&quot;0dp&quot;</em> and <em>layout_weight=&quot;1&quot;</em> on both. No fixed widths, so it adapts to any screen.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>LinearLayout is still the right tool for simple rows and columns; the mistake is nesting four of them where one ConstraintLayout would do.</p></div>",
  "RelativeLayout": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 RelativeLayout</strong><br><span>Position views relative to each other &mdash; largely superseded.</span></div><p>A <em>RelativeLayout</em> places each child relative to the parent or to a sibling: below this, to the right of that, aligned with the other. It was the standard before ConstraintLayout.</p><p>You will meet it in existing projects, which matters for the maintenance work in Module 16. For new layouts, ConstraintLayout does the same job with more capability and better performance.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Position with <em>layout_below</em>, <em>layout_toRightOf</em>, <em>layout_alignParentTop</em>.</li><li>Referenced siblings need ids.</li><li>Circular references cause a runtime exception, not a compile error.</li><li>Largely replaced by ConstraintLayout for new work.</li><li>Still common in older codebases you may be asked to maintain.</li><li>Converting to ConstraintLayout is offered by right-clicking the root in the editor.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Two views each positioned below the other is a circular dependency. It compiles cleanly and throws at runtime, which is a good illustration of why ConstraintLayout's editor-time constraint checking is an improvement.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Recognising RelativeLayout is a practical necessity for freelance maintenance work, even though you are unlikely to write a new one.</p></div>",
  "CardView": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 CardView</strong><br><span>A raised, rounded container that groups related content.</span></div><p>A <em>CardView</em> draws its children on a rounded, elevated surface. It is the standard way to present a discrete item &mdash; a product, a message, a summary &mdash; particularly as a RecyclerView row.</p><p><em>cardElevation</em> controls the shadow, <em>cardCornerRadius</em> the rounding, and <em>cardBackgroundColor</em> the surface. Keep elevation modest; every card competing for prominence achieves none.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><em>cardElevation</em> sets the shadow depth.</li><li><em>cardCornerRadius</em> rounds the corners.</li><li>Commonly used as the root of a RecyclerView row layout.</li><li>Add internal padding &mdash; content touching the card edge looks unfinished.</li><li>Keep elevation consistent; varied depths look accidental.</li><li><em>MaterialCardView</em> adds theming and a checked state.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">A product list where each row is a CardView with an image, a name and a price reads as a set of distinct items. The same content in a plain LinearLayout reads as an undifferentiated wall of text.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Grouping related content visually is what makes a dense list scannable, and it is the most visible difference between an app that looks finished and one that does not.</p></div>",
  "M4:Material Design Components": "<div class=\"lesson-note-intro\"><strong>📖 M4:Material Design Components</strong><br><span>Google's component library, so you are not building buttons from scratch.</span></div><p>Material Components provide ready-made, themed widgets: <em>MaterialButton</em>, <em>TextInputLayout</em>, <em>MaterialCardView</em>, <em>FloatingActionButton</em>, <em>Chip</em>, <em>BottomNavigationView</em>.</p><p>The practical benefit is <em>TextInputLayout</em>, which wraps an EditText and provides a floating label, helper text, character counter and an error slot &mdash; all things you would otherwise implement by hand and worse.</p><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Add the Material dependency and use a Material theme.</li><li><strong>TextInputLayout</strong> gives floating labels, helper text and error display.</li><li><strong>MaterialButton</strong> supports filled, outlined and text styles.</li><li><strong>FloatingActionButton</strong> marks the screen's primary action.</li><li>Components pick up colours from the theme &mdash; define them once.</li><li>Consistent components make an app look professional at very little cost.</li></ul></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Example</h4><div class=\"lesson-note-example\">Showing a validation error with <em>TextInputLayout</em> is one line: <em>tilEmail.error = &quot;Enter a valid email&quot;</em>. It renders in the right place, in the right colour, and clears itself when the user corrects the field.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-note-section\"><h4>Why this matters</h4><p>Using the component library rather than styling raw views by hand is the fastest way to make an app look like it was built by someone who knew what they were doing.</p></div>"
  };
})();
